;; ============================================================================

;; FUNCTIONS AND CALLS  —  port of Simple's FunNode, ParmNode, CallNode,
;;                         CallEndNode, FunPtrNode, EscapeNode
;; ============================================================================
;;
;; RESERVED-NAME HAZARD: `call` and `block` are Coil builtins. Nothing here may be named `call`;
;; every entry point is `call-*` or `*-call`.
;;
;; ============================================================================
;; THE ONE IDEA: A Fun IS a Region. A Parm IS a Phi.
;; ============================================================================
;;
;; Simple's `FunNode extends RegionNode`, and `ParmNode extends PhiNode`. That is not a code-reuse
;; convenience — it is the design, and almost everything below is a consequence:
;;
;;   - A function's inputs are the CALL SITES that reach it. It is a merge, exactly like any other.
;;   - A parameter is a Phi over those inputs. Its type is the meet of what every reaching caller
;;     passes, computed by the SAME `compute` every Phi uses.
;;   - Therefore INTERPROCEDURAL TYPE INFERENCE IS NOT A PASS. It falls out of the ordinary
;;     dataflow solver, because a parameter is already a merge and the solver already handles
;;     merges. This is what makes the closed-world argument affordable.
;;   - Therefore INLINING IS NOT A PASS EITHER. See below.
;;
;; If we invent a different call representation, all three of those properties have to be rebuilt
;; by hand as bespoke passes. Do not.
;;
;; ============================================================================
;; INLINING  —  Simple's `CallEndNode.maybeInline` / `doInline`, and `IterPeeps.iterate`
;; ============================================================================
;;
;; ## It is not a pass; it is a second worklist in the peephole fixpoint
;;
;; `IterPeeps` carries a worklist of CallEnd nodes alongside the ordinary node worklist, and ONE
;; loop interleaves them:
;;
;;     loop:
;;       run peepholes to a fixpoint          (everything that does not grow the code)
;;       pop inline candidates until one FIRES (first come, first served — no ranking)
;;       if one fired: goto loop              (re-clean, then look again)
;;       if none fired and nothing ever fired this round: done
;;       otherwise: re-queue the DEFERRED candidates and go round again
;;
;; Note there is no cost model choosing an order. Simple's own comment: "no real heuristic, first
;; come, first served". The precision comes from the call graph SCCP discovered, not from ranking.
;;
;; ## Trivial inlining CLONES NOTHING — read this twice
;;
;; Simple's comment: "Inlining does not need to clone code, merely triggers folding the Call/Fun
;; and Return/CallEnd away." The entire operation is:
;;
;;     cend._folding = true
;;     fun._folding  = true
;;     fun.setDef(1, call.ctrl())   ; the Fun's control now bypasses the Call
;;     fun.ret().setDef(3, null)    ; the Return forgets its Fun
;;     re-queue fun, cend, and their neighbours (and the Parms' users, one layer down)
;;     invalidate the idepth caches en masse
;;
;; ...and then it STOPS. The ordinary peepholes do the rest, because a Fun is a Region and a Parm
;; is a Phi:
;;
;;     the Fun now has ONE input        -> Region rule 3 collapses it into the caller's control
;;     each Parm now has ONE value      -> Phi rule 1 collapses it into the caller's argument
;;
;; Argument substitution, control splicing and return merging are all just those two existing
;; rules firing. There is no inliner body to write.
;;
;; ## Cloning is a PRE-STEP, not a second mechanism
;;
;; When the callee has other callers, a trivial fold would steal the body from them. So `_inline==2`
;; first: unlink the call, `fun.copyBody()`, build a fresh FunPtr over the clone with an
;; INLINE-ONLY function index (one that is never written to an object file), relink the call to the
;; clone — and then run the SAME trivial inline on it. One mechanism, always.
;;
;; ## The decision is a tri-state that DEFERS rather than declines
;;
;;    1  inline now, trivially
;;    2  clone the body first, then inline trivially
;;   -1  NOT NOW, but might become a candidate — re-queued via `addDep` on whatever blocked it
;;   -2  never; stop asking
;;
;; That `-1` is our own "deferred, never declined" law appearing in Simple's code. What produces it:
;;
;;   - `nIns()!=2` — not exactly one linked target yet. SCCP may still link one.
;;   - the function pointer is not yet a CONSTANT, non-null TypeFunPtr. `addDep(fptr)`.
;;   - the arguments do not yet `isa` the formals. `addDep(arg)`.
;;   - the body is currently too big, but cleanup might shrink it below the cap.
;;   - the callee is self-recursive right now — but might lose the recursive edge. `addDep(fun)`.
;;
;; And what produces `-2` (never): the CallEnd is already folding; input 0 is not a Call (malformed,
;; mid-death); the name ends in `_noInline`; or the body's approximate size already exceeds the cap.
;;
;; ## The heuristic, in full
;;
;;   - size cap 100 nodes; 200 for a constructor/initialiser that is not a class body;
;;   - NEVER inline a self-recursive function — that is loop unrolling by another name;
;;   - require exactly ONE linked target with a constant, non-null function pointer;
;;   - require every argument's type to `isa` its formal, against the recursively upgraded formal
;;     so that deep mutability requirements participate before inlining.
;;
;; ## Consequence for JSL — there is no JSL inlining question
;;
;; A JSL definition lowers to an ordinary Fun graph. It inlines through this same CallEnd worklist,
;; this same tri-state, and this same fold. `JsAdd` on two proven integers therefore specialises to
;; a single Add through mechanisms that already exist for user functions. That is the property that
;; makes the JSL design work, and it is why there is no JSL-specific machinery downstream of
;; src/jsl/lower.coil. See docs/FRONTEND.md.
;;
;; ============================================================================
;; THE CALL GRAPH IS DISCOVERED, NOT DECLARED
;; ============================================================================
;;
;; During optimistic SCCP (src/codegen/opto.coil), as a Call becomes REACHABLE — its control is
;; neither TOP nor XCONTROL — it LINKS against the function pointers reaching it: an edge Call->Fun
;; and an edge Return->CallEnd. Because a Fun is a Region and a Parm is a Phi, argument and return
;; types then propagate along ordinary edges with no special rule.
;;
;; The result is a precise call graph in linear time, computed by the same solver as everything
;; else. Under the closed-world assumption it is COMPLETE, and that completeness is what upgrades a
;; TypeScript annotation from a hint to a fact we can discharge.
;;
;; Before machine lowering the calls are UNLINKED again (the Unlink phase), because the machine
;; representation wants explicit call sequences rather than a merge.
;;
;; ============================================================================
;; THE NODES
;; ============================================================================
;;
;; FUN      a Region whose inputs are call sites. Carries a signature and a function index (fidx).
;; PARM     a Phi on a Fun, with a declared parameter type as its constraint.
;; CALL     a block TAIL and a multi: control, memory, arguments, and the function pointer LAST.
;; CALLEND  a block HEAD and a multi: the result and the new memory project out of it.
;; FUNPTR   a constant naming a Fun. `TypeFunPtr` carries a SET of function indices, so a call site
;;          whose set is a singleton is a direct call.
;; ESCAPE   a reference leaving the analysed region; nothing may be assumed about it afterwards.
;;
;; ============================================================================
;; STATUS: implemented. Fun-as-Region, Parm-as-Phi, Call, CallEnd, FunPtr, linking, the tri-state
;; decision, and trivial inlining by folding. Owed and named at the foot of this file: the CLONE
;; path (`_inline == 2`), and memory threading through calls.
;; ============================================================================
(module aot.node.call)
(import "coil.primitive" :as primitive)
(import "coil.alloc" :as alloc :use [unwrap-ptr create box!])
(import "coil.arraylist" :use *)
(import "aot.util.panic" :use *)
(import "aot.node.node" :use *)
(import "aot.node.cfg" :use *)
(import "aot.node.control" :use *)
(import "aot.node.phi" :use *)
(import "aot.type.type" :use *)

;; The inline decision, matching Simple's byte return exactly.
(const INLINE-NEVER -2)

(const INLINE-DEFER -1)

(const INLINE-TRIVIAL 1)

(const INLINE-CLONE 2)

;; Simple's size caps.
(const INLINE-MAX-SIZE 100)

(const INLINE-MAX-SIZE-INIT 200)

;; Call input layout, matching Simple exactly:
;;   0 = control, 1 = memory, 2.. = arguments, LAST = the function pointer.
;;
;; The memory slot is present and null until the memory unit lands. Keeping the layout means memory
;; drops in without renumbering every argument index — and argument indices are shared with `Parm`,
;; so a renumbering would silently rewire every parameter.
(const CALL-CTRL 0)

(const CALL-MEM 1)

(const CALL-ARG0 2)

;; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
;; FUN — a Region whose inputs are the CALL SITES that reach it
;;
;; Input 1 is either `Start` — the UNKNOWN-CALLER hook, meaning "anyone at all might call this" — or
;; a real call site. Inputs 2.. are the linked callers.
(defstruct FunNode
  [(hdr NodeHdr)
   (sig i64)  ; the signature; sharpens over time as the Return's type improves
   (name (slice u8))
   (folding bool)  ; set while this Fun is being trivially inlined away
   (approx-size i64)  ; approximate body size, the inlining heuristic
   (ret-nid i64)])  ; the Return, by nid — a back-pointer that survives edge surgery

(defn fun-sig [(f (dyn NodeOps))] (-> i64)
  (.sig (primitive/cast (ptr FunNode) (hdr f))))

(defn fun-name [(f (dyn NodeOps))] (-> (slice u8))
  (.name (primitive/cast (ptr FunNode) (hdr f))))

(defn fun-folding? [(f (dyn NodeOps))] (-> bool)
  (.folding (primitive/cast (ptr FunNode) (hdr f))))

(defn fun-approx-size [(f (dyn NodeOps))] (-> i64)
  (.approx-size (primitive/cast (ptr FunNode) (hdr f))))

(defn fun-set-folding! [(f (dyn NodeOps)) (v bool)] (-> i64)
  (do (set! (.folding (primitive/cast (ptr FunNode) (hdr f))) v) 0))

;; The signature only ever SHARPENS. `n-unlock!` first: the signature participates in nothing today,
;; but the Fun is a Region whose GVN identity depends on its edges, and Simple unlocks here too.
(defn fun-set-sig! [(f (dyn NodeOps)) (sig i64)] (-> i64)
  (if (= (fun-sig f) sig)
      0
      (do
        (n-unlock! f)
        (set! (.sig (primitive/cast (ptr FunNode) (hdr f))) sig)
        (code-add! f)
        0)))

(defn fun-ret [(f (dyn NodeOps))] (-> (Option (dyn NodeOps)))
  (arena-node (.ret-nid (primitive/cast (ptr FunNode) (hdr f)))))

;; Cannot build the Return and the Fun at once; one has to come first. So the link is a second step,
;; exactly as in Simple.
(defn fun-set-ret! [(f (dyn NodeOps)) (ret (dyn NodeOps))] (-> i64)
  (do (set! (.ret-nid (primitive/cast (ptr FunNode) (hdr f))) (n-nid ret)) 0))

;; >>> THE UNKNOWN-CALLER HOOK <<<
;;
;; While input 1 is `Start`, this function might be called by ANYONE. Its parameters can be anything,
;; so it reports CONTROL unconditionally and its Parms report their declared types.
;;
;; Removing that hook is what the closed world buys: once SCCP has proven the complete set of callers
;; and the function does not escape, the hook goes and the parameter types become the meet of what
;; the actual callers pass. That is the moment a TypeScript annotation becomes provable.
(defn fun-unknown-callers? [(f (dyn NodeOps))] (-> bool)
  (and (>= (n-nins f) 2)
       (match (n-in-safe f 1) (Some [v] (= (n-op v) OP-START)) (None [] false))))

;; Simple's `FunNode.compute`: CONTROL while callers are unknown, else the meet over real callers.
;; The Start hook is SKIPPED in the meet — it is a hook, not a caller.
(defn fun-compute [(f (dyn NodeOps))] (-> i64)
  (if (fun-unknown-callers? f)
      TY-CTRL
      (let [cnt (n-nins f)
            (mut i) 1
            (mut t) TY-XCTRL]
        (loop (if (>= (load i) cnt)
                  (break 0)
                  (do
                    (match (n-in-safe f (load i))
                      (Some [v]
                            (if (= (n-op v) OP-START)
                                0
                                (set! (mut t) (ty-meet (load t) (n-ty v)))))
                      (None [] 0))
                    (set! (mut i) (+ (load i) 1))
                    (continue))))
        (load t))))

(defn fun-idealize [(f (dyn NodeOps))] (-> (Option (dyn NodeOps)))
  ;; 1. A linked caller died. Skip input 1 while it is the unknown-caller hook: dropping the hook is
  ;; a separate, deliberate decision (see below), not something dead-path removal may do.
  (match (region-dead-path! f (fun-unknown-callers? f))
    (Some [x] (Some x))
    (None []
          ;; 2. Upgrade the signature from the actual return type, when it sharpened.
          (match (fun-ret f)
            (Some [ret]
                  (let [rt (n-ty ret)]
                    (if (and (ty-tuple? rt) (> (ty-tuple-len rt) 1))
                        (let [actual (ty-tuple-at rt 1)
                              declared (ty-sig-ret (fun-sig f))]
                          (if (and (!= actual declared) (ty-isa? actual declared))
                              (do (fun-set-sig! f (fun--sig-with-ret (fun-sig f) actual)) (Some f))
                              (fun--maybe-collapse f)))
                        (fun--maybe-collapse f))))
            (None [] (fun--maybe-collapse f))))))

;; >>> THE COLLAPSE THAT IS INLINING <<<
;;
;; A Fun that is FOLDING and down to one input, with no Phis (its Parms having collapsed already),
;; becomes that input. This is Region rule 3, and it is the second half of what makes trivial
;; inlining work: the Fun's control becomes the caller's control.
;;
;; Guarded on `folding`, because a Fun with one caller that is NOT being inlined must keep its shape —
;; it is still a function, and something may call it again.
(defn fun--maybe-collapse [(f (dyn NodeOps))] (-> (Option (dyn NodeOps)))
  (if (fun-unknown-callers? f)
      (None [(dyn NodeOps)])
      (if (and (and (fun-folding? f) (= (n-nins f) 2)) (not (region-has-phi? f)))
          (n-in f 1)
          (None [(dyn NodeOps)]))))

;; Replace the return member of a signature tuple.
(defn fun--sig-with-ret [(sig i64) (ret i64)] (-> i64)
  (let [n (ty-tuple-len sig)]
    (if (= n 1)
        (ty-tuple-of2 ret ret)  ; no args: keep the arity, only the return matters
        (if (= n 2)
            (ty-tuple-of2 ret (ty-tuple-at sig 1))
            (if (= n 3)
                (ty-tuple-of3 ret (ty-tuple-at sig 1) (ty-tuple-at sig 2))
                (do
                  (unimplemented "fun--sig-with-ret: arity above 2"
                                 "src/node/call.coil — widen with the tuple arity")
                  sig))))))

(impl NodeOps FunNode
  (hdr [(self (ptr FunNode))] (-> (ptr NodeHdr)) (primitive/cast (ptr NodeHdr) self))
  (label [(self (ptr FunNode))] (-> (slice u8)) (.name self))
  (is-const? [(self (ptr FunNode))] (-> bool) false)
  (no-const-fold? [(self (ptr FunNode))] (-> bool) true)
  (is-pinned? [(self (ptr FunNode))] (-> bool) true)
  (binop-copy [(self (ptr FunNode)) (lhs (dyn NodeOps)) (rhs (dyn NodeOps))]
              (-> (Option (dyn NodeOps)))
              (None [(dyn NodeOps)]))
  (compute [(self (ptr FunNode))] (-> i64) (fun-compute (primitive/make-dyn NodeOps self)))
  (idealize [(self (ptr FunNode))]
            (-> (Option (dyn NodeOps)))
            (fun-idealize (primitive/make-dyn NodeOps self)))
  ;; Never equal while callers are unknown, for the same reason an in-progress Region is not: two
  ;; half-known functions are not interchangeable.
  (eq-extra [(self (ptr FunNode)) (other (dyn NodeOps))]
            (-> bool)
            (if (!= (n-op other) OP-FUN)
                false
                (not (fun-unknown-callers? (primitive/make-dyn NodeOps self)))))
  (hash-extra [(self (ptr FunNode))] (-> i64) 173))

(impl CFGOps FunNode
  (cfg-node [(self (ptr FunNode))] (-> (dyn NodeOps)) (primitive/make-dyn NodeOps self))
  (block-head? [(self (ptr FunNode))] (-> bool) true)
  ;; A Fun is a Region: its dominator is the LCA of its live callers.
  (cfg-idom [(self (ptr FunNode))]
            (-> (Option (dyn NodeOps)))
            (region-idom (primitive/make-dyn NodeOps self)))
  (cfg-idepth [(self (ptr FunNode))]
              (-> i64)
              (region-idepth (primitive/make-dyn NodeOps self))))

;; Build a Fun with the unknown-caller hook already installed.
(defn fun-make [(start (dyn NodeOps)) (sig i64) (name (slice u8))] (-> (dyn NodeOps))
  (let [a (alloc/malloc-allocator)
        p (alloc/unwrap-ptr [FunNode] (alloc/create [FunNode] a))
        d (primitive/make-dyn NodeOps p)]
    (set! (.sig p) sig)
    (set! (.name p) name)
    (set! (.folding p) false)
    (set! (.approx-size p) 0)
    (set! (.ret-nid p) 0)
    (n-init! d OP-FUN)
    (n-add-def! d (None [(dyn NodeOps)]))  ; slot 0 unused, as in Region
    (n-add-def! d (Some start))  ; slot 1: the unknown-caller hook
    (invalidate-cfg!)
    (n-set-ty! d TY-CTRL)
    d))

;; Drop the unknown-caller hook. THE closed-world move: from here the parameter types are the meet of
;; what the actual callers pass, rather than "anything at all".
(defn fun-drop-unknown-callers! [(f (dyn NodeOps))] (-> i64)
  (if (not (fun-unknown-callers? f))
      (broken "fun-drop-unknown-callers!: there is no unknown-caller hook to drop")
      (do (region-del-path! f 1) 0)))

;; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
;; PARM — a Phi on a Fun, with a declared parameter type
;;
;; Input 0 is the Fun. Inputs 1.. are the matching argument from each caller, positionally aligned
;; with the Fun's inputs — which is why `region-del-path!` must count a Parm as a Phi.
;;
;; `_idx` maps one-to-one onto Call input indices, so `Parm 0` reads `Call.in(CALL-ARG0 + 0)`.
;;
;; ## Why interprocedural inference is not a pass
;;
;; A Parm is a Phi, so its type is the MEET of what every reaching caller passes — computed by the
;; SAME `compute` every Phi uses. There is no separate propagation step. That is the entire payoff of
;; the Fun-is-a-Region design, and it is what makes the closed-world argument affordable.
;;
;; `constrain` joins with the DECLARED type: a parameter is never wider than its annotation says,
;; and never narrower than the callers justify. Where those two agree the annotation is discharged.
(defstruct ParmNode [(hdr NodeHdr) (idx i64) (declared i64) (name (slice u8))])

(defn parm-idx [(p (dyn NodeOps))] (-> i64)
  (.idx (primitive/cast (ptr ParmNode) (hdr p))))

(defn parm-declared [(p (dyn NodeOps))] (-> i64)
  (.declared (primitive/cast (ptr ParmNode) (hdr p))))

(defn parm-fun [(p (dyn NodeOps))] (-> (dyn NodeOps))
  (n-in! p 0))

;; A Parm is IN PROGRESS exactly while its Fun has unknown callers. Simple: "Always in-progress until
;; we run out of unknown callers."
(defn parm-in-progress? [(p (dyn NodeOps))] (-> bool)
  (match (n-in-safe p 0)
    (Some [f] (if (= (n-op f) OP-FUN) (fun-unknown-callers? f) false))
    (None [] true)))

(defn parm-compute [(p (dyn NodeOps))] (-> i64)
  (match (n-in-safe p 0)
    (None [] TY-TOP)
    (Some [f]
          (if (!= (n-op f) OP-FUN)
              TY-TOP
              (if (parm-in-progress? p)
                  ;; Unknown callers: the parameter could be anything the annotation allows.
                  (parm-declared p)
                  ;; Otherwise the meet over LIVE callers, joined with the declared type.
                  (let [cnt (n-nins p)
                        (mut i) 1
                        (mut t) TY-TOP]
                    (loop (if (>= (load i) cnt)
                              (break 0)
                              (do
                                (match (n-in-safe f (load i))
                                  (Some [caller]
                                        (do
                                          (n-add-dep! caller p)
                                          (if (or (= (n-ty caller) TY-XCTRL)
                                                  (= (n-ty caller) TY-TOP))
                                              0
                                              (match (n-in-safe p (load i))
                                                (Some [v]
                                                      (set! (mut t)
                                                            (ty-meet (load t) (n-ty v))))
                                                (None [] 0)))))
                                  (None [] 0))
                                (set! (mut i) (+ (load i) 1))
                                (continue))))
                    ;; `constrain`: never wider than the annotation.
                    (ty-join (load t) (parm-declared p))))))))

(defn parm-idealize [(p (dyn NodeOps))] (-> (Option (dyn NodeOps)))
  (match (n-in-safe p 0)
    (None [] (None [(dyn NodeOps)]))
    (Some [f]
          (if (!= (n-op f) OP-FUN)
              ;; The Fun collapsed. A dead function can lose every caller before the Parm is removed,
              ;; and in that transient form there is no replacement input at all.
              (if (= (n-nins p) 1) (None [(dyn NodeOps)]) (n-in p 1))
              ;; >>> THE OTHER HALF OF INLINING <<<
              ;; While the Fun is FOLDING, run the full Phi rules — which is how a Parm collapses
              ;; into the caller's actual argument. Argument substitution IS Phi rule 1.
              (if (fun-folding? f)
                  (phi-single-unique-input p)
                  ;; Otherwise skip the Phi optimisations: a Parm's inputs are call sites, and most
                  ;; Phi rewrites would push work into every caller.
                  (None [(dyn NodeOps)]))))))

(impl NodeOps ParmNode
  (hdr [(self (ptr ParmNode))] (-> (ptr NodeHdr)) (primitive/cast (ptr NodeHdr) self))
  (label [(self (ptr ParmNode))] (-> (slice u8)) (.name self))
  (is-const? [(self (ptr ParmNode))] (-> bool) false)
  ;; While the Fun has unknown callers the Parm reports its DECLARED type, which may well be
  ;; constant. Folding it then would replace a parameter with one caller's value — the engine's
  ;; in-progress hook, reserved for exactly this.
  (no-const-fold? [(self (ptr ParmNode))]
                  (-> bool)
                  (parm-in-progress? (primitive/make-dyn NodeOps self)))
  (is-pinned? [(self (ptr ParmNode))] (-> bool) true)
  (binop-copy [(self (ptr ParmNode)) (lhs (dyn NodeOps)) (rhs (dyn NodeOps))]
              (-> (Option (dyn NodeOps)))
              (None [(dyn NodeOps)]))
  (compute [(self (ptr ParmNode))] (-> i64) (parm-compute (primitive/make-dyn NodeOps self)))
  (idealize [(self (ptr ParmNode))]
            (-> (Option (dyn NodeOps)))
            (parm-idealize (primitive/make-dyn NodeOps self)))
  (eq-extra [(self (ptr ParmNode)) (other (dyn NodeOps))]
            (-> bool)
            (if (!= (n-op other) OP-PARM)
                false
                (= (.idx self) (.idx (primitive/cast (ptr ParmNode) (hdr other))))))
  (hash-extra [(self (ptr ParmNode))] (-> i64) (+ 179 (.idx self))))

(defn parm-make [(fun (dyn NodeOps)) (idx i64) (declared i64) (name (slice u8))] (-> (dyn NodeOps))
  (let [a (alloc/malloc-allocator)
        p (alloc/unwrap-ptr [ParmNode] (alloc/create [ParmNode] a))
        d (primitive/make-dyn NodeOps p)]
    (set! (.idx p) idx)
    (set! (.declared p) declared)
    (set! (.name p) name)
    (n-init! d OP-PARM)
    (n-add-def! d (Some fun))
    ;; The Fun already has its unknown-caller hook at input 1, so the Parm needs a matching
    ;; placeholder to stay positionally aligned.
    (n-add-def! d (None [(dyn NodeOps)]))
    (n-set-ty! d declared)
    d))

;; Find the Parm with this index on a Fun. There is at most one.
(defn fun-parm [(f (dyn NodeOps)) (idx i64)] (-> (Option (dyn NodeOps)))
  (let [cnt (n-nouts f)
        (mut i) 0
        (mut found) (None [(dyn NodeOps)])]
    (loop (if (>= (load i) cnt)
              (break 0)
              (do
                (match (n-out f (load i))
                  (Some [u]
                        (if (and (= (n-op u) OP-PARM) (= (parm-idx u) idx))
                            (set! (mut found) (Some u))
                            0))
                  (None [] 0))
                (set! (mut i) (+ (load i) 1))
                (continue))))
    (load found)))

;; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
;; FUNPTR — a constant naming a Fun
;;
;; Its type is a `TypeFunPtr` carrying the signature and a SINGLETON function-index set, so a call
;; through it is a direct call.
(defstruct FunPtrNode [(hdr NodeHdr) (fptr-ty i64)])

(impl NodeOps FunPtrNode
  (hdr [(self (ptr FunPtrNode))] (-> (ptr NodeHdr)) (primitive/cast (ptr NodeHdr) self))
  (label [(self (ptr FunPtrNode))] (-> (slice u8)) "FunPtr")
  ;; It IS a constant — of function-pointer type. Saying so stops the engine trying to replace it
  ;; with a Con, which would loop.
  (is-const? [(self (ptr FunPtrNode))] (-> bool) true)
  (no-const-fold? [(self (ptr FunPtrNode))] (-> bool) false)
  (is-pinned? [(self (ptr FunPtrNode))] (-> bool) false)
  (binop-copy [(self (ptr FunPtrNode)) (lhs (dyn NodeOps)) (rhs (dyn NodeOps))]
              (-> (Option (dyn NodeOps)))
              (None [(dyn NodeOps)]))
  (compute [(self (ptr FunPtrNode))] (-> i64) (.fptr-ty self))
  (idealize [(self (ptr FunPtrNode))] (-> (Option (dyn NodeOps))) (None [(dyn NodeOps)]))
  (eq-extra [(self (ptr FunPtrNode)) (other (dyn NodeOps))]
            (-> bool)
            (if (!= (n-op other) OP-FUNPTR)
                false
                (= (.fptr-ty self) (.fptr-ty (primitive/cast (ptr FunPtrNode) (hdr other))))))
  (hash-extra [(self (ptr FunPtrNode))] (-> i64) (* (.fptr-ty self) 181)))

(defn funptr-make [(fun (dyn NodeOps)) (fidx i64)] (-> (dyn NodeOps))
  (let [a (alloc/malloc-allocator)
        p (alloc/unwrap-ptr [FunPtrNode] (alloc/create [FunPtrNode] a))
        d (primitive/make-dyn NodeOps p)
        t (ty-funptr (fun-sig fun) (primitive/ishl 1 fidx))]
    (set! (.fptr-ty p) t)
    (n-init! d OP-FUNPTR)
    (n-add-def! d (Some fun))
    (n-set-ty! d t)
    d))

;; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
;; CALL — a block TAIL
;;
;; Layout: 0 = control, 1 = memory, 2.. = arguments, LAST = the function pointer.
(defstruct CallNode [(hdr NodeHdr) (cend-nid i64)])

(defn call-ctrl [(c (dyn NodeOps))] (-> (dyn NodeOps))
  (n-in! c CALL-CTRL))

(defn call-fptr [(c (dyn NodeOps))] (-> (dyn NodeOps))
  (n-in! c (- (n-nins c) 1)))

(defn call-nargs [(c (dyn NodeOps))] (-> i64)
  (- (n-nins c) 3))

(defn call-arg [(c (dyn NodeOps)) (i i64)] (-> (dyn NodeOps))
  (n-in! c (+ CALL-ARG0 i)))

(defn call-cend [(c (dyn NodeOps))] (-> (Option (dyn NodeOps)))
  (arena-node (.cend-nid (primitive/cast (ptr CallNode) (hdr c)))))

(defn call-set-cend! [(c (dyn NodeOps)) (e (dyn NodeOps))] (-> i64)
  (do (set! (.cend-nid (primitive/cast (ptr CallNode) (hdr c))) (n-nid e)) 0))

(impl NodeOps CallNode
  (hdr [(self (ptr CallNode))] (-> (ptr NodeHdr)) (primitive/cast (ptr NodeHdr) self))
  (label [(self (ptr CallNode))] (-> (slice u8)) "Call")
  (is-const? [(self (ptr CallNode))] (-> bool) false)
  (no-const-fold? [(self (ptr CallNode))] (-> bool) true)
  (is-pinned? [(self (ptr CallNode))] (-> bool) true)
  (binop-copy [(self (ptr CallNode)) (lhs (dyn NodeOps)) (rhs (dyn NodeOps))]
              (-> (Option (dyn NodeOps)))
              (None [(dyn NodeOps)]))
  ;; A Call is reachable exactly when its control is.
  (compute [(self (ptr CallNode))]
           (-> i64)
           (n-ty (n-in! (primitive/make-dyn NodeOps self) CALL-CTRL)))
  (idealize [(self (ptr CallNode))] (-> (Option (dyn NodeOps))) (None [(dyn NodeOps)]))
  (eq-extra [(self (ptr CallNode)) (other (dyn NodeOps))] (-> bool) (= (n-op other) OP-CALL))
  (hash-extra [(self (ptr CallNode))] (-> i64) 191))

(impl CFGOps CallNode
  (cfg-node [(self (ptr CallNode))] (-> (dyn NodeOps)) (primitive/make-dyn NodeOps self))
  ;; A Call is a block TAIL; the CallEnd that follows is the head.
  (block-head? [(self (ptr CallNode))] (-> bool) false)
  (cfg-idom [(self (ptr CallNode))]
            (-> (Option (dyn NodeOps)))
            (n-in-safe (primitive/make-dyn NodeOps self) CALL-CTRL))
  (cfg-idepth [(self (ptr CallNode))]
              (-> i64)
              (cfg-idepth-default (primitive/make-dyn NodeOps self))))

;; Build a call site. Arguments are appended, then the function pointer LAST.
(defn call-make [(ctrl (dyn NodeOps))] (-> (dyn NodeOps))
  (let [a (alloc/malloc-allocator)
        p (alloc/unwrap-ptr [CallNode] (alloc/create [CallNode] a))
        d (primitive/make-dyn NodeOps p)]
    (set! (.cend-nid p) 0)
    (n-init! d OP-CALL)
    (n-add-def! d (Some ctrl))
    (n-add-def! d (None [(dyn NodeOps)]))  ; memory, until the memory unit
    (invalidate-cfg!)
    (n-set-ty! d (n-ty ctrl))
    d))

(defn call-add-arg! [(c (dyn NodeOps)) (arg (dyn NodeOps))] (-> i64)
  (n-add-def! c (Some arg)))

;; Append the function pointer. Must be LAST — `call-fptr` reads the final input, and `call-nargs`
;; counts everything between the memory slot and it.
(defn call-set-fptr! [(c (dyn NodeOps)) (fptr (dyn NodeOps))] (-> i64)
  (n-add-def! c (Some fptr)))

;; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
;; CALLEND — a block HEAD, and where the inline decision lives
;;
;; Input 0 is the Call. Inputs 1.. are the linked Returns — one per function this call site may
;; reach. Exactly one means a resolved direct call.
(defstruct CallEndNode [(hdr NodeHdr) (folding bool) (decision i64)])

(defn cend-call [(e (dyn NodeOps))] (-> (dyn NodeOps))
  (n-in! e 0))

(defn cend-folding? [(e (dyn NodeOps))] (-> bool)
  (.folding (primitive/cast (ptr CallEndNode) (hdr e))))

(defn cend-set-folding! [(e (dyn NodeOps)) (v bool)] (-> i64)
  (do (set! (.folding (primitive/cast (ptr CallEndNode) (hdr e))) v) 0))

(defn cend-decision [(e (dyn NodeOps))] (-> i64)
  (.decision (primitive/cast (ptr CallEndNode) (hdr e))))

;; The result type: a tuple of (control, value).
(defn cend-compute [(e (dyn NodeOps))] (-> i64)
  (match (n-in-safe e 0)
    (None [] (ty-tuple-of2 TY-XCTRL TY-TOP))
    (Some [c]
          (if (or (!= (n-op c) OP-CALL) (!= (n-ty c) TY-CTRL))
              (ty-tuple-of2 TY-XCTRL TY-TOP)
              (let [ft (n-ty (call-fptr c))]
                (if (not (ty-funptr? ft))
                    (if (ty-high? ft)
                        (ty-tuple-of2 TY-XCTRL TY-TOP)
                        (ty-tuple-of2 TY-CTRL TY-BOT))
                    ;; While folding, take the single linked caller's return directly.
                    (if (cend-folding? e)
                        (ty-tuple-of2 TY-CTRL (cend--linked-ret-ty e (ty-sig-ret (ty-funptr-sig ft))))
                        ;; Otherwise: the meet over linked Returns, joined with the declared return.
                        (ty-tuple-of2 TY-CTRL
                                      (cend--linked-ret-ty e (ty-sig-ret (ty-funptr-sig ft)))))))))))

;; The meet over linked Returns' value members, joined with the signature's declared return. With no
;; links yet, the declared return is the answer.
(defn cend--linked-ret-ty [(e (dyn NodeOps)) (declared i64)] (-> i64)
  (let [cnt (n-nins e)
        (mut i) 1
        (mut t) TY-TOP
        (mut any) false]
    (loop (if (>= (load i) cnt)
              (break 0)
              (do
                (match (n-in-safe e (load i))
                  (Some [r]
                        (let [rt (n-ty r)]
                          (if (and (ty-tuple? rt) (> (ty-tuple-len rt) 1))
                              (do
                                (set! (mut any) true)
                                (set! (mut t) (ty-meet (load t) (ty-tuple-at rt 1))))
                              0)))
                  (None [] 0))
                (set! (mut i) (+ (load i) 1))
                (continue))))
    (if (load any) (ty-join (load t) declared) declared)))

(impl NodeOps CallEndNode
  (hdr [(self (ptr CallEndNode))] (-> (ptr NodeHdr)) (primitive/cast (ptr NodeHdr) self))
  (label [(self (ptr CallEndNode))] (-> (slice u8)) "CallEnd")
  (is-const? [(self (ptr CallEndNode))] (-> bool) false)
  (no-const-fold? [(self (ptr CallEndNode))] (-> bool) true)
  (is-pinned? [(self (ptr CallEndNode))] (-> bool) true)
  (binop-copy [(self (ptr CallEndNode)) (lhs (dyn NodeOps)) (rhs (dyn NodeOps))]
              (-> (Option (dyn NodeOps)))
              (None [(dyn NodeOps)]))
  (compute [(self (ptr CallEndNode))] (-> i64) (cend-compute (primitive/make-dyn NodeOps self)))
  ;; Simple's CallEnd has no `idealize` at all — the inline decision is driven by the SEPARATE inline
  ;; worklist, not by the ordinary peephole. Keeping that split is what rations inlining to one per
  ;; round, with a full cleanup between.
  (idealize [(self (ptr CallEndNode))] (-> (Option (dyn NodeOps))) (None [(dyn NodeOps)]))
  (eq-extra [(self (ptr CallEndNode)) (other (dyn NodeOps))] (-> bool) (= (n-op other) OP-CALLEND))
  (hash-extra [(self (ptr CallEndNode))] (-> i64) 193))

(impl CFGOps CallEndNode
  (cfg-node [(self (ptr CallEndNode))] (-> (dyn NodeOps)) (primitive/make-dyn NodeOps self))
  ;; A CallEnd IS a block head.
  (block-head? [(self (ptr CallEndNode))] (-> bool) true)
  (cfg-idom [(self (ptr CallEndNode))]
            (-> (Option (dyn NodeOps)))
            (n-in-safe (primitive/make-dyn NodeOps self) 0))
  (cfg-idepth [(self (ptr CallEndNode))]
              (-> i64)
              (cfg-idepth-default (primitive/make-dyn NodeOps self))))

(defn callend-make [(c (dyn NodeOps))] (-> (dyn NodeOps))
  (let [a (alloc/malloc-allocator)
        p (alloc/unwrap-ptr [CallEndNode] (alloc/create [CallEndNode] a))
        d (primitive/make-dyn NodeOps p)]
    (set! (.folding p) false)
    (set! (.decision p) INLINE-DEFER)
    (n-init! d OP-CALLEND)
    (n-add-def! d (Some c))
    (call-set-cend! c d)
    (invalidate-cfg!)
    (n-set-ty! d (compute p))
    d))

;; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
;; LINKING — the call graph is DISCOVERED, not declared
;;
;; Simple's explanation, worth keeping: the Call->Fun edges "are not in from the start because they
;; are *too many*; we would get O(n^2) edges. So all along we have been treating them as virtual CFG
;; edges — a FunNode taking a Start input is treated as if every call on the planet might call it.
;; Now we make these CG edges concrete, as we discover that a call calls a particular function."
;;
;; A link is TWO edges:
;;   Call  -> Fun      (the Fun gains a caller; every Parm gains that caller's argument)
;;   Return-> CallEnd  (the CallEnd gains a return to meet)
;;
;; Because a Fun is a Region and a Parm is a Phi, argument types then flow into the callee through
;; the ORDINARY merge rules, with no interprocedural machinery.
(defn call-link! [(c (dyn NodeOps)) (fun (dyn NodeOps))] (-> i64)
  (do
    ;; The Fun gains a caller.
    (n-add-def! fun (Some c))
    ;; EVERY Parm gains the matching argument, in the SAME operation — the Region/Phi arity
    ;; invariant. A Parm left one value short reads the wrong caller's argument.
    (let [cnt (n-nouts fun)
          (mut i) 0]
      (loop (if (>= (load i) cnt)
                (break 0)
                (do
                  (match (n-out fun (load i))
                    (Some [u]
                          (if (= (n-op u) OP-PARM)
                              (let [idx (parm-idx u)]
                                (if (< idx (call-nargs c))
                                    (n-add-def! u (Some (call-arg c idx)))
                                    ;; The caller passes fewer arguments than the callee declares.
                                    ;; That is an arity error, not something to paper over with a
                                    ;; default: link nothing and let the type check report it.
                                    (n-add-def! u (None [(dyn NodeOps)]))))
                              0))
                    (None [] 0))
                  (set! (mut i) (+ (load i) 1))
                  (continue)))))
    ;; The CallEnd gains the callee's Return.
    (match (call-cend c)
      (Some [e] (match (fun-ret fun) (Some [r] (n-add-def! e (Some r))) (None [] 0)))
      (None [] 0))
    (invalidate-cfg!)
    (code-add! fun)
    (code-add! c)
    0))

;; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
;; THE INLINE DECISION  —  Simple's `CallEndNode.maybeInline` / `inlineCandidate`
;;
;; A TRI-STATE that DEFERS rather than declines:
;;
;;   INLINE-TRIVIAL ( 1)  inline now, by folding
;;   INLINE-CLONE   ( 2)  clone the body first, then fold      (owed — see the foot of this file)
;;   INLINE-DEFER   (-1)  NOT NOW, but might become one — re-queued when the blocker changes
;;   INLINE-NEVER   (-2)  never; stop asking
;;
;; `-1` is our "deferred, never declined" law appearing in Simple's own code. Each deferral registers
;; a dependency on whatever blocked it, so nothing has to remember to come back.

;; Decide WITHOUT acting, setting no flags. Split out because the expensive-assert path must be able
;; to ask the question with no side effects.
(defn callend-inline-candidate [(e (dyn NodeOps))] (-> i64)
  (if (cend-folding? e)
      INLINE-NEVER  ; already folding, and will collapse anyway
      (match (n-in-safe e 0)
        (None [] INLINE-NEVER)  ; malformed, mid-death
        (Some [c]
              (if (!= (n-op c) OP-CALL)
                  INLINE-NEVER
                  ;; Not exactly ONE linked target yet. SCCP may still link one, so DEFER.
                  (if (!= (n-nins e) 2)
                      INLINE-DEFER
                      (callend--candidate-with-target e c (n-in! e 1))))))))

(defn callend--candidate-with-target [(e (dyn NodeOps)) (c (dyn NodeOps)) (ret (dyn NodeOps))] (-> i64)
  (match (return-fun ret)
    (None [] INLINE-DEFER)
    (Some [fun]
          ;; SELF-RECURSION is never inlined — that is loop unrolling by another name. But the
          ;; recursive edge might yet disappear, so DEFER rather than refuse forever, and depend on
          ;; the Fun so the question is reasked if its callers change.
          (if (fun-self-recursive? fun)
              (do (n-add-dep! fun e) INLINE-DEFER)
              (let [fptr (call-fptr c)
                    ft (n-ty fptr)]
                ;; The function pointer must be a CONSTANT, non-empty, singleton set. Anything else
                ;; may still sharpen, so depend on it and defer.
                (if (not (and (ty-funptr? ft) (ty-funptr-single? ft)))
                    (do (n-add-dep! fptr e) INLINE-DEFER)
                    ;; Arguments must match the formals. `call-good-args?` depends on any that do
                    ;; not yet.
                    (if (not (call-good-args? c (fun-sig fun) e))
                        INLINE-DEFER
                        ;; SIZE. Over the hard cap: never. Under it: trivial when this is the only
                        ;; caller, otherwise the clone path.
                        (if (> (fun-approx-size fun) INLINE-MAX-SIZE)
                            INLINE-NEVER
                            (if (fun-unknown-callers? fun)
                                (do (n-add-dep! fun e) INLINE-DEFER)
                                (if (> (n-nins fun) 2) INLINE-CLONE INLINE-TRIVIAL))))))))))

;; The Fun a Return belongs to. Simple keeps it as the Return's fourth input; ours is the back-link
;; the Fun holds, walked in reverse.
(defn return-fun [(ret (dyn NodeOps))] (-> (Option (dyn NodeOps)))
  (let [cnt (n-nouts ret)
        (mut i) 0
        (mut found) (None [(dyn NodeOps)])]
    (loop (if (>= (load i) cnt)
              (break 0)
              (do
                (match (n-out ret (load i))
                  (Some [u] (if (= (n-op u) OP-FUN) (set! (mut found) (Some u)) 0))
                  (None [] 0))
                (set! (mut i) (+ (load i) 1))
                (continue))))
    (match (load found)
      (Some [f] (Some f))
      ;; No output edge: fall back to the Fun that names this Return.
      (None [] (arena-find-fun-by-ret (n-nid ret))))))

;; Find the Fun whose recorded Return is this nid.
(defn arena-find-fun-by-ret [(ret-nid i64)] (-> (Option (dyn NodeOps)))
  (let [cnt (arena-node-count)
        (mut i) 1
        (mut found) (None [(dyn NodeOps)])]
    (loop (if (> (load i) cnt)
              (break 0)
              (do
                (match (arena-node (load i))
                  (Some [n]
                        (if (and (= (n-op n) OP-FUN) (not (n-dead? n)))
                            (match (fun-ret n)
                              (Some [r] (if (= (n-nid r) ret-nid) (set! (mut found) (Some n)) 0))
                              (None [] 0))
                            0))
                  (None [] 0))
                (set! (mut i) (+ (load i) 1))
                (continue))))
    (load found)))

;; Does this Fun call itself? Simple walks the Fun's caller inputs looking for one inside the Fun.
(defn fun-self-recursive? [(f (dyn NodeOps))] (-> bool)
  (let [cnt (n-nins f)
        (mut i) 1
        (mut yes) false]
    (loop (if (or (>= (load i) cnt) (load yes))
              (break 0)
              (do
                (match (n-in-safe f (load i))
                  (Some [caller]
                        (if (= (n-op caller) OP-CALL)
                            (match (call-cend caller)
                              (Some [ce] (if (fun--reaches? f ce) (set! (mut yes) true) 0))
                              (None [] 0))
                            0))
                  (None [] 0))
                (set! (mut i) (+ (load i) 1))
                (continue))))
    (load yes)))

;; Is `ce` inside `f`'s body? Walks the dominator chain up looking for the Fun.
(defn fun--reaches? [(f (dyn NodeOps)) (ce (dyn NodeOps))] (-> bool)
  (let [(mut cur) (Some ce)
        (mut yes) false
        (mut guard) 0]
    (loop (if (> (load guard) 100000)
              (do (broken "fun--reaches?: dominator walk did not converge") (break 0))
              (match (load cur)
                (None [] (break 0))
                (Some [n]
                      (if (n-same? n f)
                          (do (set! (mut yes) true) (break 0))
                          (do
                            (set! (mut cur) (cfg-idom-of n))
                            (set! (mut guard) (+ (load guard) 1))
                            (continue)))))))
    (load yes)))

;; Does every argument's type `isa` its formal? Depends on any that does not yet, so the candidate is
;; retried when it sharpens.
(defn call-good-args? [(c (dyn NodeOps)) (sig i64) (dep (dyn NodeOps))] (-> bool)
  (let [n (ty-sig-nargs sig)]
    (if (!= (call-nargs c) n)
        false  ; arity mismatch: an error, never inlinable
        (let [(mut i) 0
              (mut ok) true]
          (loop (if (or (>= (load i) n) (not (load ok)))
                    (break 0)
                    (do
                      (let [arg (call-arg c (load i))
                            formal (ty-sig-arg sig (load i))]
                        (if (ty-isa? (n-ty arg) formal)
                            0
                            (do (n-add-dep! arg dep) (set! (mut ok) false) 0)))
                      (set! (mut i) (+ (load i) 1))
                      (continue))))
          (load ok)))))

;; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
;; TRIVIAL INLINING — read this twice: it CLONES NOTHING
;;
;; Simple's comment: "Inlining does not need to clone code, merely triggers folding the Call/Fun and
;; Return/CallEnd away." The entire operation is:
;;
;;     cend.folding = true
;;     fun.folding  = true
;;     fun.setDef(1, call.ctrl())    ; the Fun's control now BYPASSES the Call
;;     re-queue fun, cend, their neighbours, and the Parms' users one layer down
;;     invalidate the idepth caches
;;
;; ...and then it STOPS. The ORDINARY peepholes do the rest, because a Fun is a Region and a Parm is
;; a Phi:
;;
;;     the Fun now has ONE input   -> Region rule 3 collapses it into the caller's control
;;     each Parm has ONE value     -> Phi rule 1 collapses it into the caller's argument
;;
;; Argument substitution, control splicing and return merging are all just those two existing rules
;; firing. There is no inliner body to write.
;;
;; The Parms' users must be re-queued one layer down, because a Parm collapsing changes the types of
;; everything reading it and those nodes are two steps from the Fun.
(defn callend-do-trivial-inline! [(e (dyn NodeOps)) (fun (dyn NodeOps))] (-> i64)
  (let [c (cend-call e)]
    (cend-set-folding! e true)
    (fun-set-folding! fun true)
    ;; Bypass the Call: the Fun's single caller edge becomes the caller's own control.
    (n-set-def! fun 1 (Some (call-ctrl c)))
    ;; Re-queue the neighbourhood, and the Parms' users one layer down.
    (code-add! fun)
    (code-add! e)
    (code-add-outputs! fun)
    (let [cnt (n-nouts fun)
          (mut i) 0]
      (loop (if (>= (load i) cnt)
                (break 0)
                (do
                  (match (n-out fun (load i))
                    (Some [u] (if (= (n-op u) OP-PARM) (code-add-outputs! u) 0))
                    (None [] 0))
                  (set! (mut i) (+ (load i) 1))
                  (continue)))))
    (code-add-outputs! e)
    (code-add-inputs! e)
    ;; Inlining blows every cached idepth past the inline point. Bump the version rather than hunting
    ;; for the stale ones.
    (invalidate-cfg!)
    0))

;; Decide and ACT. Returns the decision; performs the inline when it is positive.
(defn callend-maybe-inline! [(e (dyn NodeOps))] (-> i64)
  (let [d (callend-inline-candidate e)]
    (if (mid-assert?)
        d  ; an expensive assert must not mutate
        (do
          (set! (.decision (primitive/cast (ptr CallEndNode) (hdr e))) d)
          (if (= d INLINE-TRIVIAL)
              (match (n-in e 1)
                (Some [ret]
                      (match (return-fun ret)
                        (Some [fun] (callend-do-trivial-inline! e fun))
                        (None [] 0)))
                (None [] 0))
              0)
          d))))

;; --- owed, and NOT stubs ---
;;
;; THE CLONE PATH (`INLINE-CLONE`). When the callee has OTHER callers, a trivial fold would steal the
;; body from them. Simple first unlinks the call, copies the body (`fun.copyBody()`), builds a fresh
;; FunPtr over the clone with an INLINE-ONLY function index — one never written to an object file —
;; relinks the call to the clone, and then runs the SAME trivial inline on it. One mechanism, always;
;; cloning is a pre-step, not a second path.
;;
;; It needs a general graph copier, which is also what the serializer and `Fun.copyBody` want, so it
;; lands with the compilation-unit work rather than being written twice. Until then a multi-caller
;; function reports INLINE-CLONE and is not inlined — correct, just weaker.
;;
;; MEMORY THREADING. `CALL-MEM` is a real slot carrying null. When the memory unit lands, a Call
;; passes the memory state in and the CallEnd projects the new one out; nothing here renumbers.

;; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
;; The control kinds this module owns, contributed to `cfg-of`
;;
;; `Fun`, `Call` and `CallEnd` are control. `Parm` is NOT — it is a Phi, and Phis are data.
(defn cfg-of-calls [(n (dyn NodeOps))] (-> (Option (dyn CFGOps)))
  (let [op (n-op n)]
    (if (= op OP-FUN)
        (Some (primitive/make-dyn CFGOps (primitive/cast (ptr FunNode) (hdr n))))
        (if (= op OP-CALL)
            (Some (primitive/make-dyn CFGOps (primitive/cast (ptr CallNode) (hdr n))))
            (if (= op OP-CALLEND)
                (Some (primitive/make-dyn CFGOps (primitive/cast (ptr CallEndNode) (hdr n))))
                (None [(dyn CFGOps)]))))))

;; Install the extension. `aot.node.boot` calls this; reaching a call node's control question before
;; it runs is a named panic, not a silent "not control".
(defn ctrl-install-call-ext! [] (-> i64)
  (set-cfg-ext! (primitive/fnptr-of cfg-of-calls)))
